The '''Korean alphabet''' is the modern [[writing system]] for the [[Korean language]]. It has gone by a [[Names of the Korean alphabet|variety of names]], such as '''{{tlit|ko|mr|Chosŏn'gŭl}}'''{{Efn|{{Korean/auto|조선글|ko_ipa=tsʰo.sʰɔn.ɡɯɭ}}}} in [[North Korea]], '''''Hangul'''''<!-- Do not wrap this in tlit; it is an English-language word -->{{efn|{{IPAc-en|lang|ˈ|h|ɑː|n|g|ʊ|l}} {{Respell|HAHN|guul}}}} internationally, and '''{{tlit|ko|rr|Hangeul}}'''{{Efn|{{Korean|한글}}; {{IPA|ko|ha(ː)n.ɡɯɭ|audio=Ko-한글.oga|label=}}}} in [[South Korea]]. The script's original name was '''{{tlit|ko|rr|Hunminjeongeum}}'''<!-- Do not link the Hunminjeongeum article here; that article is about the book, not the alphabet. -->.{{efn|{{Korean|hangul=훈민정음|hanja=訓民正音|labels=no}}}}

Before Hangul's creation, Korea had been using [[Hanja]] ([[Chinese characters]]) since antiquity. As Hanja was poorly suited for representing the Korean language, and because its difficulty contributed to high [[literacy|illiteracy]], Joseon king<!-- not capitalized per [[MOS:PSEUDOTITLE]] --> [[Sejong the Great]] ({{reign|1418|1450}}) moved to [[Origin of Hangul|create Hangul]]. The script was announced around late 1443 to early 1444 and officially published in 1446 via the text ''[[Hunminjeongeum]]'' and its companion commentary ''[[Hunminjeongeum Haerye]]''. While Hangul saw gradual adoption among both the elite and commoners, it was looked down upon by the elite for centuries. It only began to receive societal acceptance in the late 19th century. It is now the predominant script for Korean in both Koreas and among the [[Korean diaspora]]. It is also used to write the [[Jeju language]], and to a limited degree, the [[Cia-Cia language]] of Indonesia.

[[Hangul orthography]] has changed over time and differs between North and South Korea. Modern Korean language orthographies use 24{{efn|24 is the letter count used by international and South Korean scholars. North Korea considers the alphabet to have 40 letters, although both Koreas use the same set of letters. {{crossreference|See {{slink|#Letter counts}}.}}}} basic letters, which are called ''jamo''. The 14 consonants can be combined amongst themselves to represent additional and composite sounds; the same principle applies for the 10 vowels. Such combinations yield 27 additional letters, resulting in a total of 51. They are arranged in syllable blocks consisting of an initial consonant, a vowel, and an optional final consonant. The syllables can be arranged in [[Horizontal and vertical writing in East Asian scripts|vertical or horizontal rows]], although the latter practice has become dominant. [[Korean punctuation|Hangul punctuation]] is now largely similar to Western punctuation, with some differences. [[Space (punctuation)|Spaces]] between words or phrases are a modern feature of Hangul.

[[Origin of Hangul#Official narrative for letter design|Hangul letters were designed]] to be graphically simple, and traditionally consisted of only straight lines, dots, and circles. The shapes of 5 basic consonants are based on those of [[Place of articulation|human speech organs]]. Most of the other basic consonants, which are considered to correspond to "harsher" sounds than those 5, are derived by adding additional lines to those letters to indicate progressively harsher sounds. There are a [[Origin of Hangul#Hypothesized inspirations for Hangul|number of other hypothesized inspirations for the letter shapes]], but these are still debated.

The script has received significant praise from international linguists and historians. It is now a major point of pride for Korean people.

== Names ==
{{Main|Names of the Korean alphabet}}
The Korean alphabet has been referred to by various names since its invention. Its original name was ''Hunminjeongeum'' ({{Korean/auto|hangul=^훈민_정음|hanja=訓民正音|mr=yes|lit=Correct Sounds for the Instruction of the People|labels=no}}). It shared this name with [[Hunminjeongeum|the manuscript that introduced it]].{{Sfn|홍윤표|2019|pp=63–64}}

Internationally, the script goes by ''Hangul''. This spelling has been adopted as a word in the English language<ref name="MW">{{cite web |title=Hangul |url=https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/hangul |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220218052113/https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/hangul |archive-date=18 February 2022 |access-date=15 August 2017 |website=[[Merriam-Webster]]}}</ref> and is used by organizations like the [[International Organization for Standardization]] (which gives it the four-letter code <code>Hang</code> and numeric code <code>286</code>).<ref>{{Cite web |title=ISO 15924 Code Lists: Codes for the representation of names of scripts |url=https://unicode.org/iso15924/iso15924-codes.html |access-date=2025-09-23 |website=[[Unicode]]}}</ref> ''Hangul'' is an ad-hoc romanization of the name South Korea uses for the script; South Korea's preferred [[Revised Romanization of Korean|Revised Romanization]] system renders this as ''Hangeul'' ({{Lang|ko|한글}}). The [[McCune–Reischauer]] and related romanization systems render this as ''Han{{'}}gŭl''. Without diacritics, this is rendered as ''Hangul''. The name means ''script of Han'', where ''Han'' is one of the [[names of Korea]].{{Sfn|Cho|Whitman|2019|p=30}} The name appeared some time around the early 1910s.{{Sfn|홍윤표|2019|pp=38, 68}}<ref name=":2">{{Cite encyclopedia |author=김민수 |script-title=ko:한글모죽보기 |encyclopedia=[[Encyclopedia of Korean Culture]] |url=https://encykorea.aks.ac.kr/Article/E0061516 |access-date=2025-10-02 |publisher=[[Academy of Korean Studies]] |language=ko |archive-date=June 20, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250620230710/https://encykorea.aks.ac.kr/Article/E0061516 |url-status=live }}</ref>

In North Korea, the name ''Hangul'' was briefly used until it was replaced by ''Chosŏn'gŭl'' in 1949. This is in part due to differing preferences for names of Korea: North Korea refers to the whole of Korea as ''Chosŏn'', while South Korea uses ''Hanguk''.<ref name=":0">{{Cite magazine |author=홍윤표 |date=June 2018 |script-title=ko:한글 사용에 남북한은 어떠한 차이가 있을까? |trans-title=What differences are there in the use of Hangul between North and South Korea? |url=https://www.hangeul.go.kr/webzine/201806/sub1_1.html |access-date=2025-09-23 |magazine=한박웃음 |publisher=[[National Hangeul Museum]] |language=ko |issue=59}}</ref>

The script also historically went by a variety of other names, including ''ŏnmun'' ({{Korean|hangul=언문|hanja=諺文|labels=no|lit=vernacular script}}),{{sfnm|1a1=Kim-Renaud|1y=2000|1pp=17–18|2a1=Pratt|2y=2007|2p=122|3a1=Ramsey|3y=1992|3p=44|4a1=Song|4y=2006|4pp=47–48}} ''panjŏl'' ({{Korean|hangul=반절|hanja=反切|labels=no}}),{{Sfn|홍윤표|2019|pp=66–67}} and {{Korean/auto|hangul=국문|hanja=國文|mr=yes|lit=national script|labels=no|order=mhz|out=yes}}.{{Sfn|홍윤표|2019|p=67}}

== Classifications ==
Hangul is a [[Writing system#Classification by basic linguistic unit|phonographic script]]: a writing system where [[Grapheme|graphemes]] represent the sounds of a language. It is also an [[alphabet]].{{Sfn|Sampson|1985|p=120}} The script is often described as "syllabic" because of how its letters are grouped into syllables {{crossreference|(see {{slink|#Syllable blocks}})}},{{sfnm|1a1=Lee|1a2=Ramsey|1y=2011|1p=122|2a1=Kim|2y=1997|2p=152}} although linguist [[John DeFrancis]] is skeptical of this.{{Sfn|DeFrancis|1989|p=192}} Several linguists have instead argued for calling it an "alphabetic syllabary", "syllabic alphabet", or "alpha-syllabary".{{Sfnm|1a1=Pae|1y=2011|1pp=104, 114|2a1=Taylor|2a2=Taylor|2y=2014|2p=198}} While Hangul was originally a more [[Phonemic orthography|phonemic]] script (where spelling is strictly tied to pronunciation), modern Hangul has become more [[Morphophonemic orthography|morphophonemic]] (where some pronunciation changes are not reflected in spelling) over time {{crossreference|(see {{slink|Hangul orthography|Buncheol vs. yeoncheol debate}})}}.{{sfnm|1a1=DeFrancis|1y=1989|1pp=193–194|2a1=Lee|2y=1997|2pp=19–23}}

Hangul and mixed script are considered to have a property called ''[[ŏnmun ilch'i]]'' ({{Korean|hangul=언문일치|hanja=言文一致|labels=no|lit=spoken–written agreement}}): tight correspondence between pronunciation and text. By contrast, the [[Idu script|Idu]] and [[Kugyŏl]] scripts for writing Korean do not have this property.{{Efn|Idu and Kugyŏl sometimes match single Chinese characters to multisyllabic Korean words.{{sfn|Ahn|2018|p=251}}}}{{Sfnm|1a1=Ahn|1y=2018|1p=252|2a1=Pae|2a2=Winskel|2a3=Kim|2y=2025a|2p=37}} Pae, Winskel, and Kim argue this property was initially stronger but weakened over time due to changes in the language and script.{{Sfn|Pae|Winskel|Kim|2025a|p=37}}

=== Featural script ===
Some scholars argue that Hangul is what is called a "[[Featural writing system|featural script]]": a writing system where the shapes of the symbols encode phonological features of the spoken language they represent. The term was coined by Sampson in a 1985 book, wherein he argued Hangul was featural. This argument is largely based on the ''Haerye''{{'}}s explanations for the derivations of the letter shapes.{{sfnm|1a1=Sampson|1y=1985|1pp=40–42|2a1=Park|2y=2015|2p=22|3a1=Cho|3a2=Whitman|3y=2019|3pp=31–32|4a1=Meletis|4y=2025|4p=524}}

In a 1989 book, DeFrancis praises Hangul but questions Sampson's classification of Hangul as featural. He argues that Hangul encodes too few features of Korean and that most people literate in Hangul do not actively learn or process the featural principles used to construct the shapes of the letters.{{sfn|DeFrancis|1989|pp=196–198}} In a 1997 book, Chin-Woo Kim rebuts DeFrancis's argument. He argues that DeFrancis relies on a count of Korean's features that is too high, and that other scholars provide lower counts. He also argues that, even if one accepts that most do not learn or perceive Hangul's featural aspects, that does not mean such aspects do not exist. Kim also claims that scholars argue Hangul is not a featural script because it does not neatly abide by [[Distinctive feature|Jakobsonian distinctive features]].{{Sfn|Kim|1997|pp=145, 148–150}} In the same book, linguist [[Young-Key Kim-Renaud]] argues against a featural label. She argues it should be considered that the derivation rules are applied to varying quality and that some symbols represent or contribute to multiple significantly different sounds. Kim-Renaud gives the example of the dot: the dot is used as a vowel ({{Lang|ko|ㆍ}}) and a component in other vowels (e.g. two of them in {{Lang|ko|ㅑ}}). When used as a component, it does not carry the meaning of the dot vowel.{{Sfn|Kim-Renaud|1997b|p=184}} Linguists Pae, Winskel, and Kim argue that the featural designation is difficult to [[Falsifiability|falsify]], debatable, and uncertain.{{Sfn|Pae|Winskel|Kim|2025a|pp=33–35}} Linguists Taylor and Taylor argue that not all of Hangul's letters are derived from articulatory features and their shape communicates little to learners and users of Korean.{{Sfn|Taylor|Taylor|2014|p=198}} Linguist Dimitrios Meletis feels that the label is given too much attention, and that the stroke addition rule and philosophical concepts behind the letters are not purely featural.{{sfn|Meletis|2025|pp=524–525}}

== History ==
{{Main|History of Hangul}}

=== Background ===
Before the invention of Hangul, Korea had been using [[Hanja]] ([[Chinese characters]]) since antiquity. The difficulty of the script limited its use to mostly upper-class people; commoners were largely illiterate.{{sfnm|1a1=Sohn|1y=2001|1p=122|2a1=Haarmann|2y=1993|2pp=144–145}} Hanja is not well suited for representing the [[Korean language]]; the Chinese and Korean [[Language family|languages are not closely related]] and differ in significant ways.{{sfnm|1a1=Wang|1y=2023|1pp=70–71|2a1=Haarmann|2y=1993|2p=145|3a1=Kim-Renaud|3y=2000|3p=18}} Korean pronunciation and ideas could only be indirectly represented.{{Sfnm|1a1=Lee|1a2=Ramsey|1y=2011|1pp=1–2|2a1=Kim|2y=2005|2pp=3, 7–9|3a1=Oh|3y=2013|3pp=140–142}}

=== Origin ===
{{Main|Origin of Hangul}}
[[File:Hunminjeongum.jpg|thumb|First page of the ''[[Hunminjeongeum]]'', which formally introduced Hangul]]
Due to a lack of records, it is unknown when work on Hangul first began, nor what that process looked like.{{Sfn|Ledyard|1990|p=13}} Joseon king [[Sejong the Great]] ({{Reign|1418|1450}}) was responsible for Hangul's creation, and most scholars believe he was significantly personally involved in creating it.{{sfnm|1a1=Lee|1y=2009|1p=14|2a1=Ahn|2y=2004|2p=6}} Hangul was first introduced, likely in a mostly complete form, to Sejong's court in the 12th month of 1443 of the [[Korean calendar]] (around December 30, 1443 to January 28, 1444 in the [[Gregorian calendar]]).{{sfnm|1a1=Ledyard|1y=1998|1pp=85, 134–136|2a1=Yoo|2y=2006|2p=32}} Work then began on applying the script and developing official documentation for it. Part of this effort resulted in the creation of ''[[Yongbiŏch'ŏn'ga]]'', the first ever piece of Hangul literature.{{Sfn|Paek|2011|p=3}}

In the 9th month of 1446 ([[Hangul Day#Creation date of Hangul|the specific day of publication is not known]]), Hangul was officially promulgated via the introductory texts ''[[Hunminjeongeum]]'' and ''[[Hunminjeongeum Haerye]].{{sfnm|1a1=Ledyard|1y=1998|1pp=161–162|2a1=Ramsey|2y=1992|2pp=44–45}}'' The ''Hunminjeongeum'' begins with this now famous preface by Sejong:{{sfnm|1a1=Ledyard|1y=1998|1p=170|2a1=Wang|2y=2014|2p=60|3a1=Kim-Renaud|3y=2000|3p=17}}
{{Blockquote|text=The sounds of our country's language are different from those of the [[Names of China|Middle Kingdom]] and are not confluent with the sounds of characters. Therefore, among the ignorant people, there have been many who, having something they want to put into words, have in the end been unable to express their feelings. I have been distressed because of this, and have newly designed twenty-eight letters, which I wish to have everyone practice at their ease and make convenient for their daily use.{{sfnm|1a1=Ledyard|1y=1998|1p=170|2a1=Kim|2y=2000|2p=7|3a1=Kim-Renaud|3y=2000|3p=17}}|author=Sejong the Great|source=preface|title=''Hunminjeongeum''}}
Critics of Hangul emerged soon after its introduction. They argued that a native Korean script was too far a departure from [[Sadae|Chinese civilization, which they insisted Korea should be deferent to]] in a [[Confucianism|Confucian]] manner.{{sfnm|1a1=Ledyard|1y=1998|1pp=150–151|2a1=Wang|2y=2014|2p=60|3a1=Yoo|3y=2006|3p=26}} Modern historians have argued that elitism and self-interest were other motivators for anti-Hangul elite; literacy in Hanja was then seen as a status symbol and general literacy was seen as potentially harming their social positions.{{Sfnm|1a1=Ledyard|1y=1998|1pp=150–152|2a1=Haarmann|2y=1993|2pp=152–154|3a1=Song|3y=2006|3pp=47–48}}

=== Spread ===
[[File:Honggildongjeon.jpg|thumb|A copy of the landmark novel ''[[Hong Gildong jeon]]'']]
While Sejong had attempted to spread Hangul throughout the government and society, his efforts had limited success.{{sfnm|1a1=Ledyard|1y=1998|1p=379|2a1=Yoo|2y=2006|2p=33}} Hangul was only rarely used during the 15th century; its use was largely centered in Seoul, among people close to the royal family and court.{{Sfn|Ledyard|1998|pp=379–380}} It only began to spread outside of Seoul in the 16th century.{{Sfn|Ahn|2018|pp=253–256}} Women and Buddhists were significant early adopters of the script. While Hangul was rarely taught in traditional Confucianist curricula, it was often taught by women in the home.{{Sfn|Paek|2011|pp=20–21}}

[[Yeonsangun of Joseon|King Yeonsangun]] ({{Reign|1495|1506}}) persecuted the use of Hangul during the 1504 {{Ill|second literati purge|ko|갑자사화}}, after learning that criticisms of him had been written in the script.{{sfnm|1a1=Park|1y=2015|1pp=82–83|2a1=Kim|2y=2005|2p=135}} In the 16th century, the government agency [[Bureau of Interpreters]] became a significant center for Hangul scholarship, with one of its members [[Ch'oe Sejin]] still being revered today for his lasting contributions to the script and Korean linguistics.{{Sfnm|1a1=Lee|1a2=Ramsey|1y=2011|1pp=111–112|2a1=Ahn|2y=2018|2p=219|3a1=Cho|3a2=Whitman|3y=2019|3pp=48–49}}

[[Hangul orthography]] experienced significant changes in the script's early history. Around the time of Hangul's promulgation, an apparent dispute between whether to use a [[Phonemic orthography|phonemic or morphophonemic orthography]] resulted in an apparent victory for the morphophonemic faction; among that faction was Sejong himself.{{sfnm|1a1=Kim|1y=2005|1pp=27–30|2a1=Lee|2y=1997|2pp=19–23|3a1=Kim-Renaud|3y=1997b|3pp=179–180|4a1=Ahn|4y=2018|4pp=282–289}} [[Obsolete Hangul letters|A number of letters gradually stopped seeing significant use]] by the late 16th century, including the base letters {{Lang|ko|[[ㅿ]]}} and {{Lang|ko|[[ㆆ]]}}.{{Sfnm|1a1=Lee|1a2=Ramsey|1y=2011|1p=142|2a1=Ledyard|2y=1998|2p=231}} By the 17th century, the letters {{Lang|ko|[[ㅇ]]}} and {{lang|ko|[[ㆁ]]}} had merged into one.{{Sfnm|1a1=Ledyard|1y=1998|1pp=213–214, 245–246|2a1=Lee|2y=1997b|2pp=110–111|3a1=Sampson|3y=1985|3pp=127–128}} {{Ill|Tone markings in Hangul|lt=Tone markings|ko|방점}} for Hangul stopped being used around the 16th century.{{sfnm|1a1=Ledyard|1y=1998|1pp=255–256|2a1=Kim-Renaud|2y=1997b|2p=176|3a1=Cho|3a2=Whitman|3y=2019|3p=49}} The chaotic 1592–1598 [[Imjin War]] caused significant disruption and inconsistencies to Hangul orthography that persisted and even worsened into the late 19th century.{{Sfn|Lee|Ramsey|2011|pp=253–254}}

Hangul popular literature and ''[[sijo]]'' poetry began to flourish around the 17th century.{{Sfn|Lee|Ramsey|2011|pp=249–250}} The landmark novel ''[[Hong Gildong jeon]]'' was published around the beginning of the century.{{Sfn|Lee|Ramsey|2011|p=250}}

=== Enlightenment and Korean Empire periods ===
[[File:독립신문 1897.04.07 금요일자.gif|thumb|''[[Tongnip sinmun]]'', the first modern Korean newspaper and the first to use pure Hangul]]
With the end of Korea's [[isolationism]] in the 1870s, an influx of foreign ideas arrived in Korea. Around this time, Hangul began to be seen as a symbol of national identity and pride. Various Hangul reform movements arose, with many persisting into the late 20th century. Collectively, these efforts are referred to as the {{Ill|Hangul Movement|ko|한국어 운동의 역사}}.{{Sfn|Traulsen|2012|p=105}} Various issues became debated, such as whether to [[Debate on the use of Korean mixed script|use Hangul only or mixed script]],{{Sfn|Lee|Ramsey|2011|pp=287–288}} whether to [[Hangul orthography#Moasseugi vs. pureosseugi debate|abandon the syllable block structure and write linearly]],{{Sfn|King|1997|pp=222–223}} and whether to [[Hangul orthography#Horizontal vs. vertical writing debate|write vertically or horizontally]].{{Sfn|홍윤표|2019|pp=88–90}}

This era saw numerous landmark firsts for Hangul. Scottish missionary [[John Ross (missionary)|John Ross]]'s landmark 1877 work ''[[Corean Primer]]'' ({{lang|ko|조선어 첫걸음}}) saw the introduction of [[Space (punctuation)|spaces]] and horizontal writing for Hangul.{{sfn|홍윤표|2019|pp=88–90}} In 1888, a [[Morse code for non-Latin alphabets#Korean|Morse code specification for Hangul]] was developed for the first time by Korean scholar {{Ill|Kim Hagu|ko|김학우}}.{{Sfn|홍윤표|2019|pp=99–101}} In 1897, the first [[Korean Braille|Braille encoding for Hangul]], called [[Pyongyang Point]] ({{Lang|ko|평양점자}}), was invented by Canadian missionary [[Rosetta Sherwood Hall]].<ref name=":3">{{Cite journal |last=Chiu |first=Tasing |date=2024-07-03 |title=The tactile reading systems in East Asia: missionaries, colonialism, and unintended consequences |url=https://doi.org/10.1080/00309230.2023.2269860 |journal=Paedagogica Historica |volume=60 |issue=4 |page=622 |doi=10.1080/00309230.2023.2269860 |issn=0030-9230}}</ref> This period also saw the [[History of newspapers in Korea|rise of newspapers in Korea]]. In 1886, the ''[[Hansŏng chubo]]'' became the first Korean newspaper to use mixed script.{{Sfn|Park|2015|p=130}} In 1896, ''[[Tongnip sinmun|The Independent]]'', one of the earliest independent modern Korean newspapers, was established. It was written in pure Hangul.{{Sfnm|1a1=Lee|1a2=Ramsey|1y=2011|1pp=288–289|2a1=Park|2y=2015|2p=130}}

On 21 November 1894, the Joseon government decreed pure Hangul to be the country's preferred script for administrative documents, although mixed script was also allowed.{{sfnm|1a1=Ahn|1y=2018|1pp=242–243|2a1=Kim|2y=2005|2p=35}} This edict was apparently enforced to varying degrees, as exclusive Hangul was rare in many documents even until the beginning of the colonial period.{{sfnm|1a1=Ahn|1y=2018|1p=243|2a1=Sohn|2y=2001|2pp=144–145}} Joseon was succeeded by the Korean Empire in 1897. On 19 July 1905, the Korean Empire approved the first official orthography for Hangul: the [[New Edition of National Writing]] ({{Korean|hangul=신정국문|hanja=新訂國文|labels=no}}).{{Sfn|Lee|Ramsey|2011|pp=289–290}} Ultimately, this orthography proved controversial and was not put into significant practice.{{sfnm|1a1=홍윤표|1y=2019|1pp=80–82|2a1=Park|2y=2015|2pp=138–139}}

=== Colonial period ===
[[File:개정한_한글_맞춤법_통일안-한글학회.jpg|thumb|307x307px|Cover of a revision of the {{Ill|Unified Hangul Orthography|ko|한글 맞춤법 통일안}}]]
In 1910, [[Korea under Japanese rule|Korea was colonized by the Empire of Japan]]. The Korean language and script saw varying degrees of repression during the colonial period.{{Sfn|King|2007|pp=206–207}} After the 1919 [[March First Movement]] protests, the Japanese colonial government temporarily eased suppression of Korean culture in a phenomenon now dubbed {{Ill|Cultural rule|lt=cultural rule|ja|文化政治|ko|문화 통치}}. Hangul use and reform significantly expanded as a result.{{Sfn|King|2007|p=207}}

This period saw significant orthographic reform efforts. The colonial [[Government-General of Chōsen]] published the first Hangul orthography to be put in common practice in 1912 (revised in 1921 and 1930).{{Sfn|King|1997|pp=239–240}} The [[Korean Language Society]] (KLS) published its landmark 1933 {{Ill|Unified Hangul Orthography|ko|한글 맞춤법 통일안}} (UHO). The orthographies of both North and South Korea are currently based on this one.{{sfnm|1a1=Ahn|1y=2018|1pp=296–299|2a1=King|2y=1997|2p=240}} These orthographies ended the use of the vowel {{Lang|ko|[[ㆍ]]}}, although that vowel's sound had long since disappeared from the language.{{sfnm|1a1=King|1y=1997|1pp=239–240|2a1=홍윤표|2y=2019|2p=74}} The KLS also established [[Hangul Day]] in 1926.<ref>{{Cite journal |author=리의도 |year=2006 |title= |script-title=ko:한글날의 유래와 발자취 |trans-title=The Origin and Footprints of Hangul Day |url=https://www.dbpia.co.kr/journal/articleDetail?nodeId=NODE01071855 |journal=나라사랑 |language=ko |issue=111 |page=78 |issn=1739-7855 |via=[[DBpia]]}}</ref> With the rise of the [[Second Sino-Japanese War]], colonial repression of Hangul and the Korean language increased. The KLS [[Korean Language Society incident|was arrested en masse and tortured]] beginning in 1942. Hangul reform activity significantly slowed until the 1945 liberation.{{Sfn|King|2007|p=208}}

The [[Korean diaspora]] also participated in significant Hangul reform efforts. [[Koryo-saram]] (Koreans of the [[Russian Empire]] and [[Soviet Union]]) published a number of orthographic reforms,{{Sfn|King|1997|pp=240–255}} although their use of Hangul declined especially after they were subjected to [[Deportation of Koreans in the Soviet Union|a 1937 forced migration]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Son |first=Zhanna |year=2020 |title=Korean-language Textbooks (Hangul) Published in the USSR (1920-1930) |url=https://publications.hse.ru/en/articles/442577231 |journal=Journal of Multiculture and Education |volume=5 |issue=1 |pages=32–33 |via=[[Higher School of Economics]] |archive-date=August 4, 2025 |access-date=November 2, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250804061339/https://publications.hse.ru/en/articles/442577231 |url-status=live }} {{Open access}}</ref> The [[Wonic Leigh Hangul typewriter|first Hangul typewriter]] was invented in 1913 by Korean-American Wonic Leigh ({{Lang|ko|이원익}}; Yi Wŏnik).{{Sfn|홍윤표|2019|pp=101–102}}

=== Liberation and division ===
1945 saw the [[liberation of Korea]] from colonial rule, as well as [[Division of Korea|its division]]. Soon after the liberation, use of [[North–South differences in the Korean language|Hangul began to diverge between the Koreas]]. Both Koreas continued orthographic reform efforts. North Korea's current {{Ill|Compendium of Korean Language Norms|ko|조선말규범집}} was published in 1966 and revised a number of times.{{Sfn|Song|2015|pp=487–488}} South Korea's [[Hangeul Orthography]] was published in 1989.{{Sfn|Ahn|2018|pp=301–302}} The South Korean language regulator [[National Institute of Korean Language]] was established in 1991.{{Sfn|Song|2015|pp=478–479}}

Soon after the liberation, both North and South Korea pushed to increase literacy, which had remained low during the colonial period. Within a decade, a significant majority of Koreans in both Koreas became literate in at least Hangul.{{sfnm|1a1=Pae|1y=2025|1p=114|2a1=King|2y=2007|2pp=210–211}} The Koreas also pushed to eliminate Hanja from writing. North Korea eliminated Hanja in 1949, although it continued to be used and taught thereafter.{{Sfn|King|2007|p=211}} South Korea oscillated between the elimination and preservation of Hanja for decades afterwards.{{Sfn|King|2007|p=211}} In the 1990s, newspapers switched to using mostly Hangul.{{Sfn|Lee|Ramsey|2011|p=289}}

The 1960s saw the rise of computers and the [[Korean language and computers|computerization of Hangul]] in South Korea.{{sfn|Park|2016|p=44}} Such efforts lacked a standard until 1974, when KS C 5601 (predecessor to the current [[KS X 1001]]) was published.{{sfn|Park|2016|p=44}} In 1995, South Korea adopted ISO 10646-1, the [[Universal Coded Character Set]], as its standard, in KS C 5700.{{Sfn|홍윤표|2019|p=113}}<ref>{{Cite web |date=2010 |title=Korean Localization |url=https://docs.oracle.com/cd/E19455-01/806-0169/6j9hsml3i/index.html |access-date=2025-10-28 |website=[[Oracle Corporation]] |archive-date=May 21, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230521063332/https://docs.oracle.com/cd/E19455-01/806-0169/6j9hsml3i/index.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

A Hangul internet slang culture has also developed. For example, single letters have been used to convey meaning, which is typically not allowed in mainstream orthographies. For example, {{Lang|ko|[[ㅋ]]}} indicates the sound of [[laughter]] and {{Lang|ko|[[ㅠ]]}} is a visual representation of [[crying]].{{Sfn|Harkness|2015|p=502}} In the late 2010s, the ''[[yaminjeongeum]]'' trend emerged. It substitutes similar looking letters and syllables in words, like substituting the nonsense word {{Korean/auto|hangul=댕댕이|rr=yes|labels=no|out=yes}} for {{Korean/auto|hangul=멍멍이|rr=yes|lit=doggy|labels=no|out=yes}}.<ref>{{Cite news |author1=고한솔 |author2=임재우 |date=2017-10-08 |script-title=ko:롬곡롬곡·댕댕이·띵곡…'야민정음' 아세요 |trans-title=Romgokromgok, daengdaengi, ttinggok... Do you know of 'yaminjeongeum'? |url=https://www.hani.co.kr/arti/society/society_general/813654.html |access-date=2025-11-02 |work=[[The Hankyoreh]] |language=ko |archive-date=May 24, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250524050925/https://www.hani.co.kr/arti/society/society_general/813654.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Lim |first=Jae-seong |date=2022-10-07 |title=[Newsmaker] What is 'daengdaengi?' Government's use of Hangeul slang stirs controversy |url=https://www.koreaherald.com/article/2973816 |access-date=2025-11-02 |work=[[The Korea Herald]] |archive-date=March 24, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250324032822/https://www.koreaherald.com/article/2973816 |url-status=live }}</ref>

== Distribution outside Korea ==
{{See also|Korean as a foreign language|Korean language#Geographic distribution|Korean diaspora}}
Hangul has remained in significant use among parts of the [[Korean Chinese|Korean diaspora in China]]. In the [[Yanbian Korean Autonomous Prefecture]] and [[Changbai Korean Autonomous County]], it is an official writing system.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Pae |first=Hye K. |date=2024 |title=Analyzing the Korean Alphabet |url=https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-3-031-49633-2 |journal=SpringerLink |language=en |page=90 |doi=10.1007/978-3-031-49633-2}}</ref> One 2004 book claimed that around 81% of the Korean population in China is literate in Hangul.{{Sfn|Brown|Yeon|2015b|p=465}} Hangul sees a moderate degree of use in Japan. In the mid 20th century, ''[[Chōsen gakkō]]'' and ''{{Ill|Kankoku gakkō|ja|韓国学校}}'', schools aligned with North and South Korea respectively, were significant centers of Hangul education in Japan. Now, a significant majority of [[Koreans in Japan|ethnic Koreans in Japan]] attend regular Japanese schools and have low Korean language ability and Hangul literacy. Otherwise, Hangul is often used for the numerous South Korean tourists, who form a significant proportion of tourists to the country. The rise of the [[Korean Wave]] around the beginning of the 21st century has corresponded with a rise in Korean language education for Japanese people.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Ohe |first=Hye-Gyeong |url=https://global.oup.com/academic/product/language-communities-in-japan-9780198856610 |title=Language Communities in Japan |date=25 April 2022 |publisher=[[Oxford University Press]] |isbn=978-0-19-885661-0 |editor-last=Maher |editor-first=John C. |pages=82–90 |chapter=Korean: Transnational links of language and culture}}</ref> The Korean Wave has also impacted interest in [[Korean language education in the United States|Korean and Hangul in the United States]], with numerous universities and language learning tools like [[Duolingo]] teaching the subjects.<ref>{{Cite news |last=MacDonald |first=Joan |date=2013-12-11 |title=Interest In Learning Korean Grows With The Popularity Of The Hallyu |url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/joanmacdonald/2023/12/11/interest-in-learning-korean-grows-with-the-popularity-of-the-hallyu/ |access-date=2025-11-07 |work=[[Forbes]] |language=en}}</ref>

== Letters ==
{{See also|Hangul consonant and vowel tables|List of Hangul jamo}}
Letters in the Korean alphabet are called ''{{transliteration|ko|rr|jamo}}'' ({{Korean|자모|hanja=字母|labels=no}}).<ref name="encyko jamo" /> Hangul letters generally have more consistent pronunciations than those of other scripts.{{Sfn|Pae|2011|p=108}}

=== Letter counts ===
The current mainstream Hangul orthographies for Korean use 24 basic ''jamo'': 14 basic [[Consonant|consonants]] ({{Korean/auto|hangul=자음|hanja=子音|rr=yes|lit=child sound|labels=no}}<ref>{{Cite encyclopedia |author=최명옥 |script-title=ko:자음 (子音) |encyclopedia=[[Encyclopedia of Korean Culture]] |url=https://encykorea.aks.ac.kr/Article/E0048042 |access-date=2025-09-23 |publisher=[[Academy of Korean Studies]] |language=ko |archive-date=June 15, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250615143113/https://encykorea.aks.ac.kr/Article/E0048042 |url-status=live }}</ref>) and 10 basic [[Vowel|vowels]] ({{Korean/auto|hangul=모음|hanja=母音|rr=yes|lit=mother sound|labels=no}}<ref>{{Cite encyclopedia |script-title=ko:모음 (母音) |encyclopedia=[[Encyclopedia of Korean Culture]] |publisher=[[Academy of Korean Studies]] |url=https://encykorea.aks.ac.kr/Article/E0018535 |access-date=2025-09-23 |author=최명옥 |language=ko |trans-title=Vowels |archive-date=June 19, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250619034905/https://encykorea.aks.ac.kr/Article/E0018535 |url-status=live }}</ref>).<ref name="encyko jamo">{{Cite encyclopedia |author=강신항 |script-title=ko:자모 (字母) |encyclopedia=[[Encyclopedia of Korean Culture]] |url=https://encykorea.aks.ac.kr/Article/E0047911 |access-date=2025-09-23 |publisher=[[Academy of Korean Studies]] |language=ko |author2=유창균 |archive-date=August 12, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250812055435/http://encykorea.aks.ac.kr/Article/E0047911 |url-status=live }}</ref>{{Sfn|Pae|Winskel|Kim|2025a|p=36}} The basic letters can be modified and combined to yield 51 ''jamo'' in total. Duplications result in 5 additional consonants ({{lang|ko|ㄲ}}, {{lang|ko|ㄸ}}, {{lang|ko|ㅃ}}, {{lang|ko|ㅆ}}, {{lang|ko|ㅉ}}), and modifications and combinations result in 11 additional vowels ({{lang|ko|ㅐ}}, {{lang|ko|ㅒ}}, {{lang|ko|ㅔ}}, {{lang|ko|ㅖ}}, {{lang|ko|ㅘ}}, {{lang|ko|ㅙ}}, {{lang|ko|ㅚ}}, {{lang|ko|ㅝ}}, {{lang|ko|ㅞ}}, {{lang|ko|ㅟ}}, {{lang|ko|ㅢ}}){{Sfn|Song|2006|pp=48–51}} and 11 additional heterogeneous consonant digraphs ({{lang|ko|ㄳ}}, {{lang|ko|ㄵ}}, {{lang|ko|ㄶ}}, {{lang|ko|ㄺ}}, {{lang|ko|ㄻ}}, {{lang|ko|ㄼ}}, {{lang|ko|ㄽ}}, {{lang|ko|ㄾ}}, {{lang|ko|ㄿ}}, {{lang|ko|ㅀ}}, {{lang|ko|ㅄ}}).{{efn|In general, horizontal consonant clusters are called {{Korean/auto|hangul=병서|hanja=竝書|rr=yes|labels=no|order=rhz|out=yes}}. Digraphs that are composed of duplicate consonants (e.g. {{lang|ko|ㄲ}}) are called {{korean/auto|각자병서|各自並書|rr=yes|out=yes|order=rhz|labels=no}}. Clusters where the consonants differ are called {{korean/auto|합용병서|合用竝書|rr=yes|out=yes|order=rhz|labels=no}}.{{sfn|홍윤표|2019|pp=48–49}}}} South Korea considers Hangul to have 24 letters (only counting basic letters) and North Korea 40 (not counting 11 heterogeneous consonant digraphs).{{Sfnm|1a1=Taylor|1a2=Taylor|1y=2014|1p=184|2a1=최호철|2y=1988|2pp=47–48}}<ref name=":0" />

Hangul uses the same letters for initial and final consonants. Some letters are not typically used as final consonants, however.{{Sfn|Park|2015|pp=62–63}}

