Even though [[vowel length]] has been a feature of many historical and current [[dialects of Korean]] since the invention of Hangul, mainstream forms of Hangul have never indicated vowel length.{{sfn|Sampson|1985|pp=132–133}}

The sounds of especially the vowel letters and clusters have changed since Hangul's invention. For example, in [[Middle Korean]], {{Lang|ko|ㅐ}} was once pronounced as a [[diphthong]] of the two letters that graphically compose it: {{Lang|ko|ㅏ}} and {{Lang|ko|ㅣ}}. The same applies to many other vowels. Some vowels were even pronounced as [[Triphthong|triphthongs]], such as {{Lang|ko|ㅙ}} ({{Lang|ko|ㅗㅏㅣ}}). [[Vowel harmony]], which was strict in Middle Korean, has since become much less strict although still meaningful.{{Sfn|Lee|Ramsey|2011|p=161}}

=== Letter names ===
{{Main|Hangul letter names}}

Hangul vowels have been consistently named after the sound they produce, for example {{lang|ko|ㅑ}} is named ''ya''.{{sfnm|1a1=안경상|1y=2020|1p=46|2a1=Sohn|2y=2001|2pp=140–141|3a1=Martin|3y=1992|3p=22}}

The names of the consonants have varied across time and now between North and South Korea.{{Sfn|Sohn|2001|pp=138–139}}

In South Korea, most consonant names follow a {{lang|ko|ㅣ으}} spelling pattern, where the consonant being named appears in the beginning of the first syllable and end of the second. For example, {{Lang|ko|ㅂ}} is named ''bieup'' ({{lang|ko|비읍}}). There are three exceptions: {{lang|ko|ㄱ}} is named ''giyeok'' ({{lang|ko|기역}}), {{lang|ko|ㄷ}} is named ''digeut'' ({{lang|ko|디귿}}), and {{lang|ko|ㅅ}} is named ''siot'' ({{lang|ko|시옷}}). These inconsistencies are the result of approximate Hanja transcriptions of these names in the 16th century. South Korea decided to keep these traditional spellings; these spellings had been used for centuries and had become a part of Korean culture. Doubled consonants have the prefix ''ssang'' ({{lang|ko|쌍}}) attached to the name of the letter being doubled. For example, {{lang|ko|ㅆ}} is named ''ssangsiot'' ({{lang|ko|쌍시옷}}).<ref name="NIKLQA">{{cite web |script-title=ko:한글 자모의 수는 스물넉 자로 하고, 그 순서와 이름은 다음과 같이 정한다. |trans-title=The number of Hangul letters is 24, and their order and names are as follows |url=https://korean.go.kr/kornorms/regltn/regltnView.do#a224 |access-date=2025-09-23 |website=한국어 어문규범 |publisher=[[National Institute of Korean Language]] |language=ko |archive-date=May 24, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250524190953/https://korean.go.kr/kornorms/regltn/regltnView.do#a224 |url-status=live }}</ref>{{sfn|이관규|2024|p=43}}

North Korea decided to regularize the inconsistencies to all fit the {{lang|ko|ㅣ으}} spelling pattern. For doubled consonants, North Korea uses ''toen'' ({{Korean|hangul=된|labels=no|lit=hard}}, referring to the harder pronunciation) instead of ''ssang'' ({{Korean|hangul=쌍|hanja=雙|labels=no|lit=double}}, referring to letter shapes) for the duplicated consonants (e.g. the name of {{lang|ko|ㄲ}} is {{Korean/auto|hangul=된기윽|mr=yes|labels=no}}).<ref name=":0" />{{sfnm|1a1=최호철|1y=1988|1p=37|2a1=Martin|2y=1992|2p=22}} One reason for doing this was that ''ssang'' is a [[Sino-Korean vocabulary|Sino-Korean word]], which North Korea sometimes discourages in favor of native Korean vocabulary.<ref name=":0" />

=== Design ===
{{See also|Origin of Hangul#Official narrative for letter design}}
[[File:Pronounciation ㄱ.png|thumb|The shape of {{lang|ko|ㄱ}} resembles the shape of the tongue (red line) when pronouncing the letter's sound.]]
<!--The image Pronounciation ㅅ.png is incorrect; ㅅ is just supposed to be the incisor and not the tongue meeting the incisor. Do not use that image. Perhaps better to just use this one image for brevity's sake. -->
Hangul letters were designed to be graphically simple, and originally consisted of only straight lines, dots, and circles.{{Sfn|Kim|1983|p=85}} The ''Hunminjeongeum Haerye'' provides explanations of how the shapes of Hangul letters were derived. The shapes of 5 basic consonants are based on those of [[Place of articulation|human speech organs]] during the production of the letter's sound:{{Sfnm|1a1=Lee|1a2=Ramsey|1y=2011|1pp=116–117|2a1=Ledyard|2y=1998|2pp=243–246}}

* {{Lang|ko|[[ㄱ]]}} depicts the root of the [[tongue]] blocking the upper [[palate]]
* {{Lang|ko|[[ㄴ]]}} depicts the tongue touching the upper palate
* {{Lang|ko|[[ㅁ]]}} depicts the [[mouth]]
* {{Lang|ko|[[ㅅ]]}} depicts the [[incisor]]
* {{Lang|ko|[[ㅇ]]}} depicts the [[throat]]

From these five, most of the other consonant shapes are derived. In most cases, lines are added to the letters to represent related sounds that are considered more "severe" or "harsh". For example, {{Lang|ko|ㄱ}} ({{IPA|/k/}}) {{Arrow|r}} {{Lang|ko|[[ㅋ]]}} ({{IPA|/kʰ/}}).{{Sfnm|1a1=Lee|1a2=Ramsey|1y=2011|1pp=116–117|2a1=Ledyard|2y=1998|2pp=243–246|3a1=Kim-Renaud|3y=1997b|3p=164}}

The vowels are claimed to be designed based on concepts in [[Chinese philosophy]]:{{Sfnm|1a1=Ledyard|1y=1998|1pp=204–206, 242–243, 248–249|2a1=Lee|2a2=Ramsey|2y=2011|2pp=120–121}}

* {{Lang|ko|[[ㆍ]]}}, an archaic vowel, represents [[Yin and yang|yin]] and earth
* {{Lang|ko|[[ㅡ]]}} represents yang and the sun
* {{Lang|ko|[[ㅣ]]}} represents man (neutral)

The rest of the vowels are derived from these three letters, for example {{Lang|ko|ㅏ}} is a combination of {{Lang|ko|ㅣ}} and {{Lang|ko|ㆍ}} (the dot was originally drawn detached from the line, but has since become an attached short line).{{Sfnm|1a1=Ledyard|1y=1998|1pp=204–206, 242–243, 248–249|2a1=Lee|2a2=Ramsey|2y=2011|2pp=120–121|3a1=Kim|3y=1983|3p=85}} Other philosophical concepts are linked to the vowels and consonants, although modern scholars have expressed skepticism of the concepts, and by proxy, their link to Hangul.{{sfnm|1a1=Ledyard|1y=1998|1pp=285–286|2a1=Taylor|2a2=Taylor|2y=2014|2pp=185–186}}

The classifications of the vowels, while not believed to be philosophically meaningful, do reflect [[vowel harmony]] in especially the [[Middle Korean]] phonology of the time. In other words, yang vowels tended to occur along other yang vowels, and yin with yin. Man (neutral) vowels could go with either.{{Sfn|Lee|Ramsey|2011|pp=161–162}}

Since Hangul's creation, scholars have also argued that perhaps other scripts may have influenced Hangul as well, although this is still debated.{{sfn|Taylor|Taylor|2014|p=194}} Chinese characters almost certainly had some influence on Hangul's design, for example in the syllable block format.{{sfnm|1a1=Pae|1y=2011|1p=107|2a1=Meletis|2y=2025|2pp=531–532}} There are a [[Origin of Hangul#Hypothesized inspirations for Hangul|variety of hypotheses]], with the [[ʼPhags-pa inspiration for Hangul hypothesis|ʼPhags-pa hypothesis]] in particular having some traction among modern scholars.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Tranter |first=Nicolas |url=https://www.taylorfrancis.com/books/mono/10.4324/9781315028521/language-change-east-asia-mcauley |title=Language Change in East Asia |date= |publisher=Curzon Press |year=2001 |isbn=0-7007-1377-8 |editor-last=McAuley |editor-first=Thomas E. |edition=1st |pages=200–202 |language=en |chapter=Script 'Borrowing', Cultural Influence, and the Development of the Written Vernacular in East Asia |doi=10.4324/9781315028521 |via=[[Taylor & Francis]] |archive-date=June 6, 2024 |access-date=November 8, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240606102119/https://www.taylorfrancis.com/books/mono/10.4324/9781315028521/language-change-east-asia-mcauley |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite encyclopedia |last=Kim |first=Chin-Woo |title=Korean Script: History and Description |date=2006-01-01 |encyclopedia=[[Encyclopedia of Language and Linguistics]] |pages=239–243 |editor-last=Brown |editor-first=Keith |url=https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/B0080448542045685 |access-date=2025-08-09 |edition=2nd |place=Oxford |publisher=Elsevier |isbn=978-0-08-044854-1 |author-link=Chin-Woo Kim |archive-date=March 13, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120313094539/http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/B0080448542045685 |url-status=live }}</ref>

=== Alphabetic order ===
{{Main|Hangul alphabetical order}}

The ordering of the letters has varied across time and now between North and South Korea.

The 11,172 characters in the [[Hangul Syllables]] Unicode character block follow [[Hangul alphabetical order#KS X 1026-1|a sort order]] specified in the South Korean national standard {{ill|KS X 1026-1|ko|KS X 1026-1}}. That order accounts for the various Hangul Unicode code points, including obsolete letters.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2008 |title=An introduction to Korean Standard KS X 1026-1:2007, Hangul processing guide for information interchange |url=https://www.unicode.org/L2/L2008/08225-n3422.pdf |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210921132517/http://unicode.org/L2/L2008/08225-n3422.pdf |archive-date=21 September 2021 |access-date=17 September 2021 |publisher=[[Unicode Consortium]]}}</ref>

==== North Korean order ====
North Korea uses the following orders:

: Initial consonants: {{lang|ko|ㄱ ㄴ ㄷ ㄹ ㅁ ㅂ ㅅ ㅈ ㅊ ㅋ ㅌ ㅍ ㅎ ㄲ ㄸ ㅃ ㅆ ㅉ ㅇ}}{{efn|The initial {{lang|ko|ㅇ}} is introduced in North Korea's official {{ill|Compendium of Korean Language Norms|ko|조선말규범집}} after {{lang|ko|ㅅ}}.{{sfn|North Korea|2010|pp=4–6}} Some non–North Korean scholarly sources also use that ordering.{{sfnm|1a1=Sohn|1y=2001|1pp=146–147|2a1=Martin|2y=1992|2p=21}} This ordering places {{lang|ko|ㅇ}} at the end because, when it is an initial, it does not produce its own sound.<ref name=":0" />{{sfn|Martin|1992|pp=21–22}} In 1999, North Korea submitted a proposal to [[Unicode]] and [[ISO]] (that was eventually rejected) that places {{lang|ko|ㅇ}} at the end of the order for initial consonants.<ref name="N5999">{{cite web |url=https://unicode.org/L2/L1999/99380.htm |title=New Work item proposal (NP) for an amendment of the Korean part of ISO/IEC 10646-1:1993 |date=1999-12-07 |id=[[NCITS|L2]]/99-380, [[ISO/IEC JTC 1]] N5999 |website=[[Unicode Consortium]] |access-date=October 27, 2025 |archive-date=January 14, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250114193636/https://www.unicode.org/L2/L1999/99380.htm |url-status=live }}</ref>}}
: Vowels: {{lang|ko|ㅏ ㅑ ㅓ ㅕ ㅗ ㅛ ㅜ ㅠ ㅡ ㅣ ㅐ ㅒ ㅔ ㅖ ㅚ ㅟ ㅢ ㅘ ㅝ ㅙ ㅞ}}{{sfn|Sohn|2001|pp=146–147}}
: Final consonants: [[Zero (linguistics)|∅]]<!--Explicitly shown because it matters for sorting--> {{lang|ko|ㄱ ㄳ ㄴ ㄵ ㄶ ㄷ ㄹ ㄺ ㄻ ㄼ ㄽ ㄾ ㄿ ㅀ ㅁ ㅂ ㅄ ㅅ ㅇ ㅈ ㅊ ㅋ ㅌ ㅍ ㅎ ㄲ ㅆ}}{{sfnm|1a1=Sohn|1y=1997|1p=197|2a1=North Korea|2y=2010|2pp=4–6}}

Modifications and combinations of initial consonants and vowels are placed at the end of the order. Final consonants follow the same order as initial, but combinations of consonants are inserted into that order and are sorted by the second consonant.<ref name="NIKL1999">{{Cite news |url=https://korean.go.kr/nkview/nknews/d_199901.html |volume=6 |script-title=ko:옛 글자의 배열 순서와 북한의 자모 배열 순서 |author=이승재 |language=ko |trans-title=Ordering archaic Hangul and North Korea's order |newspaper=새국어 소식 |publisher=[[National Institute of Korean Language]] |year=1999 |archive-date=August 14, 2025 |access-date=October 27, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250814055500/https://korean.go.kr/nkview/nknews/d_199901.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

==== South Korean order ====
South Korea uses the following orders:<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.korean.go.kr/nkview/news/12/128.htm |volume=5 |script-title=ko:한글 자모의 배열 순서 |author=이승재 |language=ko |trans-title=Alphabetical order for Hangul |newspaper=새국어 소식 |publisher=[[National Institute of Korean Language]] |year=1998 |access-date=2025-10-15 |archive-date=February 26, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210226161705/https://www.korean.go.kr/nkview/news/12/128.htm |url-status=live }}</ref>{{sfn|Yeon|Brown|2011|pp=9–10}}
: Initial consonants: {{lang|ko|ㄱ ㄲ ㄴ ㄷ ㄸ ㄹ ㅁ ㅂ ㅃ ㅅ ㅆ ㅇ ㅈ ㅉ ㅊ ㅋ ㅌ ㅍ ㅎ}}
: Vowels: {{lang|ko|ㅏ ㅐ ㅑ ㅒ ㅓ ㅔ ㅕ ㅖ ㅗ ㅘ ㅙ ㅚ ㅛ ㅜ ㅝ ㅞ ㅟ ㅠ ㅡ ㅢ ㅣ}}
: Final consonants: ∅<!--See above for why this is shown--> {{lang|ko|ㄱ ㄲ ㄳ ㄴ ㄵ ㄶ ㄷ ㄹ ㄺ ㄻ ㄼ ㄽ ㄾ ㄿ ㅀ ㅁ ㅂ ㅄ ㅅ ㅆ ㅇ ㅈ ㅊ ㅋ ㅌ ㅍ ㅎ}}

Modifications and combinations of letters are placed just after the primary or initial parent letter. Such modifications and combinations are then sorted according to the previously established orderings. E.g. {{lang|ko|ㄱ}} is followed by {{lang|ko|ㄲ}} and {{lang|ko|ㅘ}} by {{lang|ko|ㅙ}}.<ref name="NIKL1999" />

=== Obsolete letters ===
{{Main|Obsolete Hangul jamo}}
{{Contains special characters|Old Hangul|section=section}}

A number of letters and letter combinations are no longer in use for transcribing Korean, although some are still actively used for transcribing other languages.{{Sfn|Cho|Whitman|2019|p=54}} This includes the base consonants {{lang|ko|[[ㆁ]]}}, {{Lang|ko|[[ㅿ]]}}, and {{Lang|ko|[[ㆆ]]}}, as well as the base vowel {{Lang|ko|[[ㆍ]]}},{{Sfn|Lee|Ramsey|2011|pp=116–119, 156}} although that last letter is still used for the [[Jeju language]].{{Sfn|Lee|Ramsey|2011|p=156}} Examples of obsolete doubled letters include {{Lang|ko|[[ㆀ]]}},{{Sfn|Lee|Ramsey|2011|p=130}} {{Lang|ko|[[ㆅ]]}},{{Sfn|Ledyard|1998|pp=226–227}} and the doubled vowel {{Lang|ko|[[ᆢ]]}}.{{Sfn|홍윤표|2019|pp=76–77}} A number of now obsolete letters were virtually exclusively used for transcribing Chinese, like the {{Ill|pure dental and palatal-supradental sibilants in Hunminjeongeum|lt=pure dental and palatal-supradental sibilants|ko|훈민정음의 치두음과 정치음}} ({{Lang|ko|ᄼ}}, {{Lang|ko|ᄽ}}, {{Lang|ko|ᅎ}}, {{Lang|ko|ᅏ}}, {{Lang|ko|ᅔ}} and {{Lang|ko|ᄾ}}, {{Lang|ko|ᄿ}}, {{Lang|ko|ᅐ}}, {{Lang|ko|ᅑ}}, {{Lang|ko|ᅕ}}){{Sfn|Osterkamp|2012|p=90}} and most of the [[Obsolete Hangul jamo#Light labial letters|light labial letters]] (e.g. {{Lang|ko|ㆄ}}, {{Lang|ko|ᄛ}}, {{Lang|ko|ㅱ}}, {{Lang|ko|ㅹ}}), although {{lang|ko|ㅸ}} was used for Korean.{{Sfn|Lee|Ramsey|2011|p=119}} Clustered consonants have also changed. Clusters mixing different letters were once acceptable for initial consonants, with some clusters even including up to three letters, like {{Lang|ko|ㅴ}}, which was used in {{Lang|ko|ᄢᅳᆷ}} ({{Lit|crack, opening}}).{{Sfn|Lee|Ramsey|2011|p=119}} A number of letters were invented in the centuries following Hangul's invention, for example {{Lang|ko|[[ᆖ]]}} (a doubled form of {{Lang|ko|ㅡ}}) was invented in the 1900s.{{Sfn|King|1997|p=221}}

== Orthography ==
{{Main|Hangul orthography}}

[[File:자귀나무 sign.jpg|thumb|A sample of Hangul writing using the South Korean orthography. Letters are arranged in syllable blocks, spacing is used, and most punctuation used is similar to that of the West, with the exception of the [[tilde]] (~) for number ranges.]]
Hangul orthography has changed over time. Since 1948, [[North–South differences in the Korean language|North and South Korea have used different orthographies]]. However, because their current orthographies are based on the 1933 {{Ill|Unified Hangul Orthography|ko|한글 맞춤법 통일안}}, they are still relatively similar. North Korea now uses the {{Ill|Compendium of Korean Language Norms|ko|조선말규범집}}{{Sfn|Song|2015|pp=487–488}} and South Korea the Hangeul Orthography.{{sfnm|1a1=Ahn|1y=2018|1pp=301–302|2a1=Song|2y=2006|2p=168}}

The [[Orthographic depth|depth of current Hangul orthographies]] is a subject of some disagreement. Many scholars argue current orthographies have a relatively [[Shallow orthographies|shallow or transparent orthography]], in that pronunciation generally closely corresponds to what is written. Their complexities are often the product of sound changes in the Korean language over time as well as [[Morphophonemic orthography|morphophonemic orthographic]] practices (changing some spellings to reflect the function or meaning of some words).{{Sfn|Kim|2025|p=377}} Some have described current orthographies as deep because of the morphophonemic practices.{{Sfn|Yang|Yang|O'Grady|2019|p=22}}

=== Syllable blocks ===
Hangul letters are arranged into syllable blocks in a principle called ''moasseugi'' ({{Korean|hangul=모아쓰기|labels=no|lit=gathering together and writing}}).{{Sfn|King|1997|p=222}} Each syllable block is broadly divided into two or three components: an initial consonant ({{Korean/auto|hangul=초성|hanja=初聲|rr=yes|labels=no}}), vowel ({{Korean/auto|hangul=중성|hanja=中聲|rr=yes|labels=no}}; also called "medial"), and optional final consonant ({{Korean/auto|hangul=종성|hanja=終聲|rr=yes|labels=no}}; also called {{Korean/auto|hangul=받침|rr=yes|lit=pedestal|labels=no}}).{{Sfnm|1a1=Cho|1a2=Whitman|1y=2019|1p=33|2a1=Lee|2a2=Ramsey|2y=2000|2pp=17–18|3a1=Martin|3y=1992|3p=6}} Final heterogenous [[Digraph (orthography)|digraph]] consonant clusters are orthographically considered to be two different letters combined, but phonologically considered to be a single letter.{{Sfn|Pae|Winskel|Kim|2025a|pp=36–37}} When no initial consonant sound is needed, the [[Zero consonant|zero initial consonant]] {{Lang|ko|ㅇ}} is used.{{Sfn|Lee|Ramsey|2000|pp=17–18}}

In modern orthographies, to construct a syllable block, the following rules are applied in order:{{sfnm|1a1=Kim-Renaud|1y=1997c|1pp=283–284|2a1=Kim-Renaud|2y=2009|2pp=10–12}}

# Write the initial consonant.
# Place the vowel depending on the appropriate condition below:
#* If the vowel has a long vertical stroke ({{Lang|ko|ㅏ}}, {{Lang|ko|ㅑ}}, {{Lang|ko|ㅓ}}, {{Lang|ko|ㅕ}}, {{Lang|ko|ㅐ}}, {{Lang|ko|ㅒ}}, {{Lang|ko|ㅔ}}, {{Lang|ko|ㅖ}}, or {{Lang|ko|ㅣ}}), write it to the right of the initial.
#* If the vowel has a long horizontal stroke ({{Lang|ko|ㅗ}}, {{Lang|ko|ㅛ}}, {{Lang|ko|ㅜ}}, {{Lang|ko|ㅠ}}, {{Lang|ko|ㅡ}}), write it below the initial.
#* If the vowel is a combination and begins with a long horizontal stroke followed by a long vertical stroke (e.g. {{Lang|ko|ㅢ}}), place the initial above the horizontal stroke.
# If there is a non-[[Zero (linguistics)|zero]] final consonant, it is written at the bottom. If the final consonant is a combination, place the letters side by side.

