Hangul letters currently used for Korean (19 initial consonants, 21 vowels, and 28 final consonants) can be combined to yield 11,172 syllables.{{Efn|This is derived from multiplying the number of initial consonants, vowels, and final consonants together. Of the total, 399 are just initial consonant and vowel pairs.{{sfn|Pae|2011|p=115}}}}{{sfnm|1a1=Park|1y=2016|1p=43|2a1=Pae|2y=2011|2p=108}} However, many of those syllables virtually never see use in natural language; scholars have argued that only around 2,000 are used in Korean.{{sfnm|1a1=Hwang|1y=2025|1p=76|2a1=Taylor|2a2=Taylor|2y=2014|2p=188}}

Several scholars have argued that the syllable block structure was likely inspired by and makes Hangul resemble the form of Chinese characters,{{sfnm|1a1=Pae|1y=2011|1p=107|2a1=Meletis|2y=2025|2pp=531–532}} and that their similar form aids compatibility in writing them together in mixed script.{{Sfn|Meletis|2025|pp=531–532}}

Several scholars have argued that the syllable block structure is a good fit for the phonology and speech cadence of Korean. Several have also argued that the limited number of syllable shapes, with vowels and consonants in consistent positions, makes Hangul easier to read and learn.{{sfnm|1a1=Meletis|1y=2025|1p=527|2a1=Taylor|2a2=Taylor|2y=2014|2pp=189–190|3a1=Winskel|3y=2025|3p=551}} Taylor and Taylor argue that the syllable block structure is especially helpful to young children learning Korean and for people with disabilities that affect reading.{{Sfn|Taylor|Taylor|2014|p=222}}

Some scholars have argued that syllable blocks have the downside of causing orthographic complexity{{Sfnm|1a1=Song|1y=2006|1pp=54–57|2a1=Lee|2a2=Ramsey|2y=2000|2pp=29–30}} and difficulty with moveable type and computers.{{sfnm|1a1=Kim-Renaud|1y=1997b|1p=178|2a1=Sampson|2y=1985|2p=144}} In especially the 19th and 20th centuries, these downsides motivated Hangul reformers to [[Hangul orthography#Moasseugi vs. pureosseugi debate|debate if the syllable block structure should be abandoned in favor of a fully linear orthography]]. Such an orthography is called ''{{ill|pureosseugi|ko|풀어쓰기}}'' ({{Korean|hangul=풀어쓰기|labels=no|lit=separate and write}}). While several ''pureosseugi'' orthographies were developed, none ever saw significant adoption.{{sfnm|1a1=King|1y=1997|1pp=222–223|2a1=Traulsen|2y=2012|2pp=120–121}}

=== Spelling ===
In general, words are spelled as they sound in the respective standard Korean dialect, with most exceptions being due to [[Phonemic orthography|morphophonemic]] word-formation rules. For example, {{Korean/auto|hangul=하늘|rr=yes|lit=sky|labels=no|order=hrl|out=yes}} is spelled the way it is pronounced; there is no need to spell it {{lang|ko|한을}}, as {{lang|ko|하늘}} is already a whole word and not a stem with particle.{{sfn|Taylor|Taylor|2014|p=205}} However, {{Korean/auto|hangul=먹어|rr=yes|lit=to eat|labels=no|order=hrl|out=yes}} has this spelling despite being pronounced {{Lang|ko|머거}}. This is because it follows a word-formation rule that preserves the spellings of the stem verb {{Lang|ko|먹}} and the particle {{Lang|ko|어}}. Other exceptions also exist, for example relating to pronunciation differences between stem words when pronounced alone and when attached to particles that begin with {{Lang|ko|ㅇ}}.{{Sfn|Taylor|Taylor|2014|pp=204–205}}

==== North–South differences ====
{{See also|North–South differences in the Korean language}}
Various shared words are spelled differently between North and South Korea. One significant reason for this is the differing [[Standard language|standard dialects]] used. North Korea uses the [[Pyongan dialect]] and South Korea the [[Gyeonggi dialect]]. Differences arise due to various phonemena, including the [[initial sound rule]], [[vowel harmony]], local pronunciations, [[Loanword|loanwords]], [[Tenseness|tensing]], [[Compound (linguistics)|compounds]], [[morphological derivation]], and the ''{{Ill|saisiot|ko|사잇소리 현상}}'' (epenthic {{Lang|ko|ㅅ}}).{{Sfn|Sohn|1997|pp=200–212}}

=== Punctuation ===
{{Main|Korean punctuation}}

Hangul has used varying punctuation over time and now in both North and South Korea. Modern Hangul now uses a mix of modern Western, Korean, and East Asian punctuation.{{Sfn|Song|2006|p=58}}

==== South Korean punctuation ====
South Korean punctuation largely follows Western practice, with some exceptions.{{Sfn|Martin|1992|pp=20–21}} Examples of exceptions include 『 』 or ≪ ≫{{efn|Using the exact characters used on the South Korean orthography website. The [[National Institute of Korean Language]] stated that it does not prescribe Unicode code points.<ref>{{Cite web |script-title=ko:[답변]기타 |url=https://www.korean.go.kr/front/onlineQna/onlineQnaView.do?mn_id=216&qna_seq=309172 |website=[[National Institute of Korean Language]] |date=2025-01-20 |access-date=2026-05-09 |archive-date=2026-05-09 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260509184935/https://www.korean.go.kr/front/onlineQna/onlineQnaView.do?mn_id=216&qna_seq=309172 |quote=한글 맞춤법에서는 문장 부호의 일반적인 생김새와 쓰임을 정하고 있으나, 전산상의 문자 코드 등과 같은 구체적인 사항은 정하고 있지 않습니다. 따라서 유니코드 등 전산상으로 구분되지만 유사한 모양을 한 여러 문자 중 어느 것을 사용할 것인가의 판단은 어문 규범의 범위를 벗어나 이곳에서 정확한 답변을 드리기 어렵습니다.}}</ref>}}; either of these sets of characters can be put around titles of works. Also, the [[tilde]] (~) can be used to indicate ranges of numbers or dates, as well as distances.<ref>{{Cite web |script-title=ko:문장 부호 |trans-title=Punctuation |url=https://korean.go.kr/kornorms/regltn/regltnView.do#a714 |access-date=2025-10-07 |website=한국어 어문 규범 |publisher=[[National Institute of Korean Language]] |language=ko |archive-date=May 24, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250524190953/https://korean.go.kr/kornorms/regltn/regltnView.do#a714 |url-status=live }}</ref>

[[Space (punctuation)|Spaces]] are used in Hangul. As with most other scripts, practices around spaces are somewhat arbitrary and ambiguous because it is difficult to define what a [[word]] is. Spacing is considered to be one of the most difficult aspects of Hangul orthography; many native Korean speakers do not use spaces properly or sometimes even at all.{{Sfn|Lee|Ramsey|2000|pp=25–26}} In general, spaces are inserted between lexical clusters called ''eojeol'' ({{Korean|hangul=어절|hanja=語節|labels=no}}). These lexical clusters can be subdivided into various other categories.<ref>{{Cite encyclopedia |author=이래호 |script-title=ko:어절 (語節) |encyclopedia=[[Encyclopedia of Korean Culture]] |url=https://encykorea.aks.ac.kr/Article/E0036073 |access-date=2025-10-24 |publisher=[[Academy of Korean Studies]] |language=ko |archive-date=June 20, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250620125602/https://encykorea.aks.ac.kr/Article/E0036073 |url-status=live }}</ref>{{Sfn|Kim|Lee|Song|Kim|2025|p=241}} Each ''eojeol'' can consist of a word and its subsequent corresponding particles or compounds. For example, "{{lang|ko|학교에 간다}}" consists of two ''eojeol'' separated by a space. The first is "{{lang|ko|학교}}" ({{lit|school}}) with an attached [[adposition]]al particle "{{lang|ko|에}}". The second is "{{lang|ko|간다}}", a [[Grammatical conjugation|conjugated verb]].{{Sfn|Kim|Lee|Song|Kim|2025|pp=239–240}}

==== North Korean punctuation ====
North Korean punctuation is similar to Western practice. Differences include the lack of Western [[Quotation mark|quotation marks]] (e.g. "), which are replaced by [[guillemet]] (≪ ≫).{{Sfn|North Korea|2010|pp=37–38}} Like South Korea, it recommends the tilde (~) for ranges of numbers or dates.{{Sfn|North Korea|2010|pp=42–43}} Recommendations are also given for vertical writing. Vertical writing punctuation is largely the same as horizontal, but guidance is given on the placement and rotation of some punctuation marks.{{Sfn|North Korea|2010|pp=45–46}}

North Korean spacing rules result in fewer spaces and more compound words when compared to South Korea's.{{Sfn|Sohn|1997|pp=210–212}}

=== Writing direction ===
{{See also|Horizontal and vertical writing in East Asian scripts}}
Historically, Hangul syllable blocks were arranged vertically (top to bottom, right to left). Over the course of the 20th century, horizontal writing (left to right, top to bottom) became dominant in both Koreas.{{Sfn|홍윤표|2019|pp=88–90}} South Korea allows both horizontal and vertical writing, while North Korea officially prefers horizontal writing.{{Sfn|Sohn|1997|pp=195–196}} Lee and Ramsey argue that the syllable block structure of Hangul aids the adaptability of the script to both directions of writing.{{Sfn|Lee|Ramsey|2000|p=20}}

== Style ==
[[File:Hangul TypeStyles sansPen.svg|thumb|150px|Several major categories of Hangul font styles]]
The [[History of Hangul#History of style|style of Hangul writing has changed over time]]. Originally, Hangul letters were drawn highly geometric and regular. They were thus awkward to write with brushes. Their style gradually changed in response to that issue.{{Sfn|Kim|1983|p=85}} Aesthetic considerations have also impacted Hangul style. The sizes and shapes of letters in syllable blocks are typically altered depending on the format of the syllable block, to make letters relatively proportionally sized.{{Sfn|Kim-Renaud|1997c|p=283}}

There are a variety of Hangul [[Font|fonts]]. [[Serif]] fonts are called [[Batang (font style)|''batang'' or ''myongjo'']]. [[Sans-serif]] fonts are called ''[[dotum]]'' or ''Gothic'' ({{Korean/auto|hangul=^고딕|rr=yes|labels=no}}).{{Sfn|Lunde|2009|p=25}}

===Stroke order===
Hangul adopted aspects of [[Stroke order|Chinese stroke order]]. Broadly, strokes of letters are written from top to bottom, left to right.{{Sfn|Kim-Renaud|1997c|p=283}}
