== Hangul and computers ==
{{Main|Korean language and computers}}

=== Encoding methods ===
Hangul has been implemented for use on [[Computer|computers]] in a number of ways. The implementations fall into two broad categories: the composition model (also called ''{{ill|Johab|ko|한글 조합형 인코딩}}'') and the precomposed model (also called ''{{ill|Wansung|ko|한글 완성형 인코딩}}''). Johab considers syllables to be a combination of letters, while Wansung renders syllables as entire fixed blocks.{{sfn|Park|2016|pp=43–44}} [[Unicode]] implements both Johab and Wansung<ref name="corespec18">{{cite book |title=The Unicode Standard Version 17.0 – Core Specification |chapter-url=https://unicode.org/versions/Unicode17.0.0/core-spec/chapter-18/ |chapter=East Asia |access-date=2025-11-07 |date=2025-09-09 |isbn=978-1-936213-35-1 |publisher=[[Unicode Consortium]] |archive-date=September 17, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250917065947/https://unicode.org/versions/Unicode17.0.0/core-spec/chapter-18/ |url-status=live }}</ref> and implements Johab using several mathematical tricks that allow for efficient composition and decomposition of syllables.<ref name="corespec18" /> In mainstream Unicode, while contemporary Hangul for Korean can all be rendered using Wansung, [[Obsolete Hangul jamo|archaic letters and syllables]] are rendered using Johab. Particular fonts may be needed to display archaic letters properly.{{sfn|양왕성|2018|pp=227–232}}

The primary encoding method for Hangul currently in use is [[Unicode]]. There are a number of other coded character set standards that were used largely historically, although they continue to see some use. The South Korean government maintains [[KS X 1001]] and [[KS X 1002]]. North Korea maintains [[KPS 9566]]. China maintains [[GB 12052]]. There is also the [[Unified Hangul Code]], a private standard by the company [[Microsoft]].{{sfn|Lunde|2009|pp=87–88, 269}} {{As of|2026}}, 96.1% of websites with the [[.kr]] domain (used by South Korea) used [[UTF-8]] encoding, with only 6.3% using an alternate standard [[EUC-KR]] (total exceeds 100%; websites can use multiple encodings).<ref>{{Cite web |title=Distribution of Character Encodings among websites that use .kr |url=https://w3techs.com/technologies/segmentation/tld-kr-/character_encoding |access-date=2026-01-22 |website=w3techs.com}}</ref>

Archaic Hangul was not fully supported in Unicode until the late 2000s. [[Private Use Areas]] were often used for that purpose until official Unicode implementation was added.{{sfn|양왕성|2018|pp=226–228}}  

