=== Hangul keyboards ===
[[File:KB South Korea.svg|thumb|The South Korean ''{{ill|dubeolsik|ko|두벌식}}'' keyboard layout]]
For typing Hangul, there are a number of keyboard layouts in use. The current most common layout is called ''{{ill|dubeolsik|ko|두벌식}}''. In this layout, consonants are on the left of the keyboard and vowels on the right, and no distinction is made between keys for initial and final consonants.{{sfn|Lunde|2009|pp=340–341}} North and South Korea have different versions of ''dubeolsik''.<ref>{{Cite journal |script-title=ko:한글 세벌식 자판의 세 가지 유형과 남북 통합 설계 |trans-title=Three Modes of Three Sets Type Korean Keyboards and Unified Designs for North and South Koreas |last=Kim |first=Kuk |year=2009 |volume=37 |issue=4 |url=https://jksqm.org/journal/view.php?number=1751 |journal=Journal of Korean Society for Quality Management |page=52 |archive-date=March 19, 2025 |access-date=November 8, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250319115440/https://www.jksqm.org/journal/view.php?number=1751 |url-status=live }}</ref> For the South Korean ''dubeolsik'' there is a designated key or keystroke used to toggle between Hangul and Latin input modes.{{sfn|Lunde|2009|pp=311, 340–341}}

== Derivatives and uses for other languages ==
[[File:Korean Braille.jpg|thumb|[[Korean Braille]] encodes the letters of Hangul]]
There are a number of communication systems that encode the letters of Hangul. Examples include [[Korean Braille]],<ref name=":3" /> [[Morse code for non-Latin alphabets#Korean|Korean morse code]],{{Sfn|홍윤표|2019|pp=99–101}} the [[Korean manual alphabet]],<ref>{{Cite encyclopedia |author=윤병천 |script-title=ko:한글 지문자 (한글 指文字) |encyclopedia=[[Encyclopedia of Korean Culture]] |url=https://encykorea.aks.ac.kr/Article/E0070741 |access-date=2025-11-04 |publisher=[[Academy of Korean Studies]] |language=ko |archive-date=May 19, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250519234446/https://encykorea.aks.ac.kr/Article/E0070741 |url-status=live }}</ref> the [[Korean spelling alphabet]],<ref>{{Cite web |script-title=ko:[별표 1] 무선통신에 사용하는 모르스부호·약어 및 통화표(제4조관련) |trans-title=[Appendix 1] Morse code, abbreviations, and terminology used in wireless communication (regarding article 4) |url=https://www.law.go.kr/LSW/admRulLsInfoP.do?admRulSeq=2200000049192 |access-date=2025-11-03 |website=Korean Law Information Center |publisher=[[Ministry of Government Legislation]] |page=47 |language=ko |archive-date=March 5, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250305222040/https://www.law.go.kr/LSW/admRulLsInfoP.do?admRulSeq=2200000049192 |url-status=live }}</ref> and Hangul [[shorthand]].{{Sfn|홍윤표|2019|pp=108–109}} Most [[Romanization of Korean|romanization systems for Korean]] work by mapping Hangul spellings to Latin letters. However, many of them are [[Transcription (linguistics)|transcription]] systems and not [[transliteration]] systems. While transliteration systems are fully [[Romanization#Methods|reversible]], meaning one can always retrieve the original Hangul spelling from romanized text, transcription systems are not.{{sfn|Lunde|2009|pp=44–45}}

=== Use for Jeju ===
{{Main|Jeju language#Orthography}}

The [[Jeju language]] was traditionally not written. Hangul has since become used to write it.{{Sfn|Yang|Yang|O'Grady|2019|pp=22–23}} Two orthographies are in current use: one created in 1991 by the Jeju Dialect Research Society ({{Lang|ko|제주방언연구회}}) and another created in 2014 by the provincial government.<ref>{{cite conference |author=강영봉 |date=16 June 2018 |title= |script-title=ko:'제주어 표기법'에 대한 단상 |trans-title=Brief Thoughts on "Jeju Language Orthography" |url=http://www.jst.re.kr/upload/board/pdf/364_1551924777898.pdf |conference=제주어 표기법 토론회 |language=ko |location= |pages=12–14 |access-date=November 3, 2025 |archive-date=September 30, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230930151443/http://jst.re.kr/upload/board/pdf/364_1551924777898.pdf |url-status=live }}</ref> Hangul orthography for Jeju is relatively similar to those for Korean, with one notable difference being the use of the vowel {{Lang|ko|[[ㆍ]]}}, which is no longer used for Korean. Spelling is attempted to be made to be morphophonemic, although there is some uncertainty around how to do this.{{Sfn|Yang|Yang|O'Grady|2019|pp=22–23}}

=== Uses for non-Koreanic languages ===
[[File:兒學編 sample.png|thumb|Entries from the 1908 text ''[[Ahakp'yŏn]]''. Here, Hangul is used to transcribe Korean, Chinese, Japanese, and English (in order, within each cell, from top right to left, then bottom).]]
Since its creation, Hangul has been used to encode the sounds of other languages. It was designed to be able to transcribe both [[Middle Korean|Korean]] and [[Late Middle Chinese|Chinese of the time]]. To accomplish this, additional consonants were created exclusively to transcribe Chinese;{{sfnm|1a1=Ledyard|1y=1998|1pp=132–133|2a1=Ramsey|2y=1992|2pp=49–50}} these consonants have often been revived or repurposed for use in other languages. Before the modern era, Hangul was also used to transcribe [[Japanese language|Japanese]], [[Ryukyuan languages|Ryukyuan]], and [[Manchu language|Manchu]].{{Sfn|Osterkamp|2012|pp=94–95, 98–100}} Beginning around the 19th century, Hangul was used to transcribe Russian<ref>{{Cite journal |last=King |first=Ross |author-link=Ross King (academic) |date=May 2024 |title=Another 'language that failed'? The beginnings of 'Soviet' Korean in the Russian Far East, 1922–1937 |url=https://www.jbe-platform.com/content/journals/10.1075/kl.00007.kin |journal=Korean Linguistics |publisher=[[John Benjamins Publishing Company]] |volume=20 |issue=1 |page=49 |doi=10.1075/kl.00007.kin |issn=0257-3784 |eissn=2212-9731 |archive-date=September 26, 2025 |access-date=November 3, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250926025329/https://www.jbe-platform.com/content/journals/10.1075/kl.00007.kin |url-status=live }}</ref> and English.{{Sfn|Osterkamp|2012|pp=88–89}}

Since 1986, the South Korean [[National Institute of Korean Language]] has provided official guidance on how to approximately render a number of non–Korean languages into South Korean standard Hangul, particularly for seamless use alongside the Korean language.<ref>{{Cite web |script-title=ko:국립국어원 외래어 표기법 |trans-title=Methods for transcribing foreign languages |url=https://www.korean.go.kr/front/page/pageView.do?page_id=P000104&mn_id=97 |access-date=2025-11-02 |website=[[National Institute of Korean Language]] |language=ko |archive-date=July 16, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250716045414/https://www.korean.go.kr/front/page/pageView.do?page_id=P000104&mn_id=97 |url-status=live }}</ref>{{sfn|Qian|2018|pp=35–36}} A number of scholars have attempted to modify Hangul to be able to represent the sounds of every language, similar to the [[International Phonetic Alphabet]].<ref>{{Cite book |last=Kim-Renaud |first=Young-Key |author-link=Young-Key Kim-Renaud |url=https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-3-030-90761-7 |title=The Two Koreas and their Global Engagements |publisher=[[Palgrave Macmillan|Palgrave Macmillan Cham]] |year=2022 |isbn=978-3-030-90761-7 |editor-last=Jackson |editor-first=Andrew David |edition=eBook |page=210 |chapter=Korean Language, Power, and National Identity |doi=10.1007/978-3-030-90761-7 |archive-date=August 11, 2024 |access-date=November 3, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240811020322/https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-3-030-90761-7 |url-status=live }}</ref> For example, [[Hyun Bok Lee]]'s [[International Korean Phonetic Alphabet]] (IKPA; {{lang|ko|한글음성문자}}).<ref name="kjd">{{cite news |url=https://koreajoongangdaily.joins.com/2008/01/18/features/Hangul-may-help-others-record-history/2885281.html|title=Hangul may help others record history|newspaper=[[Korea JoongAng Daily]]|date=2008-01-18|last=Lee|first=Eun-joo}}</ref>

Efforts have been made to make Hangul the primary script for various languages. Some South Korean linguists have been encouraging ethnic groups without scripts for their languages to adopt Hangul. In the 1990s, a Hangul-based alphabet was devised for the [[Lahu language]] of China and Southeast Asia, but this did not see significant adoption.<ref name=":02">{{Cite news |last=Wright |first=Tom |last2=Fairclough |first2=Gordon |date=12 September 2009 |title=To Save Its Dying Tongue, Indonesian Isle Orders Out for Korean |url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB125261759118600981 |access-date=27 October 2025 |work=Wall Street Journal |issn=0099-9660 |archive-date=September 1, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250901003355/https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB125261759118600981 |url-status=live }}</ref> In October 2012, a pilot program was launched for Hangul to be taught to speakers of the [[Ghari language|Ghari]] and [[Kwaraqae]] languages of the [[Solomon Islands]]. The program was halted less than a year after its launch due to a lack of funding.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Choi |first=Jae-hee |date=2023-04-09 |title=A case study: The Solomon Islands project 10 years ago |url=https://www.koreaherald.com/article/3100596 |access-date=2025-11-07 |work=[[The Korea Herald]] |archive-date=April 19, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250419212006/https://www.koreaherald.com/article/3100596 |url-status=live }}</ref>

In the late 1980s, Taiwanese linguists made at least one [[Taiwanese Hangul|adaptation of Hangul for Taiwanese Hokkien]], a [[Sinitic languages|Sinitic language]]. However, the Taiwanese government eventually endorsed the use of Chinese characters for that language.<ref name="IdealSMin">{{cite journal |last=洪惟仁 |date=2010 |title=閩南語書寫法的理想與現實 |url=http://www.twlls.org.tw/jtll/documents/5.1-5.pdf |url-status=dead |journal=臺灣語文研究 |language=zh |volume=5 |issue=1 |pages=89, 101–105 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170308190715/http://www.twlls.org.tw/jtll/documents/5.1-5.pdf |archive-date=8 March 2017 |access-date=14 January 2022 |trans-title=Idealism vs. Reality: Writing Systems for Taiwanese Southern Min}}</ref><ref name="WrittenTaiwanese">{{cite book |author1=楊允言 |url=http://ip194097.ntcu.edu.tw/memory/tgb/thak.asp?id=321 |title=台語文運動訪談暨史料彙編 |author2=張學謙 |author3=呂美親 |date=2008 |publisher=國史館 |isbn=9789860132946 |location=Taiwan |pages=284–285 |language=zh |trans-title=Compilation of Historical Materials and Interviews on the Written Taiwanese Movement |access-date=14 January 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220926161110/http://ip194097.ntcu.edu.tw/memory/TGB/thak.asp?id=321 |archive-date=26 September 2022 |url-status=dead}}</ref><ref name="TLLS">{{citation |author=Dong Zhongsi (董忠司) |title=「台灣閩南語槪論」講授資料彙編 |publisher=Taiwan Languages and Literature Society}}</ref>

==== Cia-Cia language ====
Beginning in the late 2000s, efforts began to be made by the South Korean private organization [[Hunminjeongeum Society]] to have Hangul be used for the [[Cia-Cia language]] of Indonesia. That language did not already have a script in significant use for it. While Hangul was not legally made an official script for the language due to Indonesian legal restrictions, the local government of [[Baubau]] partnered with the Hunminjeongeum Society and several other organizations to promote the use of Hangul for Cia-Cia. A number of local schools taught the script.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Lee |first=Tae-hoon |date=6 October 2010 |title=Hangeul didn't become Cia Cia's official writing |url=https://www.koreatimes.co.kr/southkorea/20101006/hangeul-didnt-become-cia-cias-official-writing |access-date=27 October 2025 |website=[[The Korea Times]]}}</ref><ref name=":22">{{Cite news |last=Choe |first=Sang-Hun |date=11 September 2009 |title=South Korea's Latest Export: Its Alphabet |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2009/09/12/world/asia/12script.html |access-date=27 October 2025 |work=[[The New York Times]] |archive-date=February 6, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180206222936/http://www.nytimes.com/2009/09/12/world/asia/12script.html |url-status=live }}</ref> The program was not financially supported by the South Korean or Indonesian governments. It experienced several difficulties in the early 2010s,<ref>{{cite news |last1=Yi |first1=Whan-woo |date=8 October 2012 |title=Sejong Institute withdrawal to leave Cia-Cia out in cold |url=https://www.koreatimes.co.kr/www/news/nation/2012/10/113_121766.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170206184347/http://www.koreatimes.co.kr/www/news/nation/2012/10/113_121766.html |archive-date=6 February 2017 |access-date=30 June 2017 |work=[[The Korea Times]]}}</ref> although it continued to operate. It saw renewed activity and support in the early 2020s.<ref name="KimSH2">{{cite news |last1=Kim |first1=So-hyun |date=2 April 2023 |title=[Hello Hangeul] Sharing the Korean alphabet with the world |url=https://www.koreaherald.com/view.php?ud=20230402000150 |access-date=5 January 2024 |work=[[The Korea Herald]] |archive-date=November 26, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241126115812/https://www.koreaherald.com/view.php?ud=20230402000150 |url-status=live }}</ref> Hangul has since continued to be taught and used in the area.<ref name="nytimes">{{Cite news |last=Suhartono |first=Muktita |date=4 November 2024 |title=An Indonesian Tribe's Language Gets an Alphabet: Korea's |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2024/11/04/world/asia/indonesia-korea-hangul.html |access-date=5 November 2024 |work=New York Times |url-status=live }}</ref>

==== Chitembo language ====

The sister of a [[Great Lakes Twa|Twa]] leader from eastern [[Democratic Republic of Congo]] visited South Korea in 2015 for a cultural-exchange event. She learned that Hangul was used for the Cia-Cia language in Indonesia, and thought it would help her people, who speak the [[Chitembo language]], estimated to be spoken by 500,000 to 700,000 people. Four South Korean linguists developed a Hangul alphabet for Chitembo over the next few years. Teachers from a Hangul school in [[Los Angeles, California]], taught the alphabet to primary-school teachers from [[Bunyakiri]] in Congo. Those school teachers, along with South Korean missionaries, in turn taught it to Twa in the region, who were mostly illiterate. {{As of|2023}}, they reported that 40 adults and 300 children had been taught the Chitembo Hangul alphabet. Children could write their names within half an hour, but the adults took months to do the same.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kim |first=So-hyun |date=April 2, 2023 |title=Hangeul as a writing system for African tribe |url=https://www.koreaherald.com/article/3095044 |access-date=2025-11-19 |website=[[The Korea Herald]] |language=en |archive-date=May 23, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250523012811/https://www.koreaherald.com/article/3095044 |url-status=live }}</ref>

== Evaluations and legacy ==
Linguist [[John DeFrancis]] wrote that "scholars are in general agreement on the excellence of the Korean alphabetic script".{{sfn|DeFrancis|1989|p=196}} Hangul has been described by various linguists and historians as the best or among the best actively used writing systems,{{sfnm|1a1=Sampson|1y=1985|1p=120|2a1=DeFrancis|2y=1989|2p=198}}<ref>{{Cite web |last=Livaccari |first=Chris |date=2025-10-24 |title=The World's Most Incredible Alphabet |url=https://asiasociety.org/education/worlds-most-incredible-alphabet |access-date=2025-10-24 |publisher=[[Asia Society]] |quote="In an interview shortly before his death in 1999, <nowiki>[</nowiki>[[James D. McCawley]]<nowiki>]</nowiki> noted that 'Hangul is the most ingeniously devised writing system that exists, and it occupies a special place in the typology of writing systems.'" |archive-date=March 20, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250320152253/https://asiasociety.org/education/worlds-most-incredible-alphabet |url-status=live }}</ref> as well as a landmark intellectual achievement in Korean and human history.<ref name=":1">{{Cite book |last=Reischauer |first=Edwin O. |author-link=Edwin O. Reischauer |url=http://archive.org/details/eastasiagreattra00reis |title=East Asia: The great tradition |last2=Fairbank |first2=John King |author-link2=John King Fairbank |date=1960 |publisher=[[Houghton Mifflin]] |isbn=978-1125640975 |publication-date=1960 |pages=434–436 |access-date=2025-10-15 |via=[[Internet Archive]]}}</ref>{{sfnm|1a1=Sohn|1y=2001|1p=129|2a1=Shin|2y=2014|2p=22}} Hangul is a significant aid for modern scholars interested in the [[historical linguistics]] of the Korean language. Scholarly understanding of historical Korean sharply improves right at the invention of Hangul.{{Sfnm|1a1=Lee|1a2=Ramsey|1y=2011|1pp=1–2|2a1=Sohn|2y=2001|2p=11|3a1=Lee|3a2=Ramsey|3y=2000|3p=4}} Several linguists have argued that Hangul is distinguished among writing systems in common use in that it was created, designed to suit the Korean language and be easy to learn, and original in a number of ways.{{Sfn|Meletis|2025|pp=529–530}}

When Westerners first began encountering Hangul in the 19th century, a number of them evaluated it glowingly and bemoaned that it was not yet respected by Koreans themselves.{{Sfn|Pae|Winskel|Kim|2025a|p=32}}<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Silva |first=David J. |date=2002 |title=Western Attitudes toward the Korean Language: An Overview of Late Nineteenth-and Early Twentieth-Century Mission Literature |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/23719764 |journal=[[Korean Studies (journal)|Korean Studies]] |volume=26 |issue=2 |pages=277–278, 282 |issn=0145-840X}}</ref> Since then, Hangul has become a major source of pride for Korean people.{{sfnm|1a1=Kim|1y=2005|1pp=1–2|2a1=King|2y=2007|2pp=220–221|3a1=Meletis|3y=2025|3pp=532–533}}

A copy of the ''Hunminjeongeum Haerye'', the document that formally introduced Hangul, was designated a [[National Treasure (South Korea)|National Treasure of South Korea]] on December 20, 1962<ref>{{Cite web |title=Hunminjeongeum (The Proper Sounds for the Instruction of the People) |url=https://english.khs.go.kr/chaen/search/selectGeneralSearchDetail.do?mn=EN_02_02&sCcebKdcd=11&ccebAsno=0000700000000&sCcebCtcd=11&pageIndex=1&region=&canAsset=&ccebPcd1=&searchWrd=HUNMINJEONGEUM&startNum=&endNum=&stCcebAsdt=&enCcebAsdt=&canceled=&ccebKdcd=&ccebCtcd= |access-date=2025-09-27 |website=[[Korea Heritage Service]]}}</ref>{{Sfn|Ahn|2018|p=5}} and a UNESCO [[Memory of the World Register – Asia and the Pacific|Memory of the World]] in 1997.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Hunminjeongum Manuscript |url=https://www.unesco.org/en/memory-world/hunminjeongum-manuscript |access-date=2025-09-27 |website=[[UNESCO]] |archive-date=March 7, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250307011634/https://www.unesco.org/en/memory-world/hunminjeongum-manuscript |url-status=live }}</ref>{{Sfn|Ahn|2018|p=5}}

